This looks like May 2022. Headline CPI at 8.56% YoY matches the April 2022 reading, which was published in May. The fed funds rate of 0.33% fits the period after the March 2022 hike, and the unemployment rate of 3.6% also matches. The 10-year yield at 2.85% and the 2-year at 2.63% fit end of April 2022 better than end of May, when the 10-year was about 2.85 and the 2-year about 2.5.

At the end of April 2022, the S&P 500 fell about 8.8% on the month, and the VIX was about 33. At the end of May, the S&P was roughly flat on the month and the VIX was about 26. A 1-month return of -10.2% is more consistent with April, as is the 10-year yield, which closed April at 2.89%. Core CPI at 6.44% matches the March 2022 reading of 6.5%, which would be the latest published at the end of April, since April CPI was not released until May 11. Headline CPI of 8.56% is close to March's 8.54%. So the snapshot is end of April 2022.

